{"mode": "normal", "text": "Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Sed tincidunt tortor ac egestas laoreet. Quisque nec odio fringilla, varius felis et, eleifend ex. Fusce luctus vitae metus ut volutpat. Donec maximus in tortor sed semper. Nunc libero augue, iaculis eu magna pellentesque, lacinia commodo lacus. Curabitur porta eu sem et lobortis. Vestibulum maximus eleifend nulla et laoreet. Interdum et malesuada fames ac ante ipsum primis in faucibus. Fusce gravida, magna quis dignissim sodales, libero elit convallis arcu, ut tristique magna sapien vitae quam. Integer metus sem, ornare in aliquet luctus, rutrum eu leo. Donec ullamcorper elit massa, eu elementum augue dignissim et.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Placeholder title", "subheading": "Placeholder subheading"}], "blockName": "", "blockType": "iconAccordions", "secondaryColorLayout": false}, "format": "", "version": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "block", "fields": {"id": "68f18898577f756260829d54", "items": [], "heading": "", "blockName": "", "blockType": "collectionItemSlider", "aspectRatio": "aspect-[1.25/1]", "overflowSetting": "md:!overflow-hidden", "usePlaceholders": true, "widthConstraint": true, "placeholderCount": 5, "placeholderImage": 2, "placeholderTitle": "Name"}, "format": "", "version": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}	690ba014819f9179409794f2	\N	container-small
3	154	version.layout	1111	{"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "INTRODUCTION", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Raw feeding is the practice of feeding your dog raw, unprocessed meats to closely mimic what their ancestors would have eaten. Raw diets are not new. They have been the primary source of nutrition for dogs for thousands of years, long before the very first dry dog biscuit was invented. Raw diets lost popularity with the convenience of commercial pet food but returned to the spotlight in the early 90s as pet owners responded to increasing health concerns in family pets. The market has only continued to grow since then with an ever-growing selection of pre-made diets and endless resources for tackling them at home. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Raw feeding isn't right for every dog or household. Many argue that dogs can tolerate the pathogenic bacteria present in raw meat because dogs naturally have a gastric pH of 1-2. This acts as their first line of defense, followed closely by the bacteria strains present in their microbiome. However, the risk of your dog contracting harmful bacteria commonly found on raw meats such as salmonella, e. coli, and listeria still exists and can spread to others in your household. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Dog parents who feed raw diets commonly report smaller and less smelly stools, healthier skin and coat, and better energy and focus. While reported cases of pets contracting pathogenic bacteria are low, many of our dogs today no longer have healthy functioning guts that are able to handle this bacteria. The FDA notes that in cases where dogs show symptoms of Salmonellosis it's generally ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"id": "690bcf6c7231316a6372932e", "type": "link", "fields": {"url": "https://www.fda.gov/animal-veterinary/animal-health-literacy/get-facts-about-salmonella", "newTab": true, "linkType": "custom"}, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "secondary to an underlying problem", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 8, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 8}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ". For pets with clearly compromised guts, a gently cooked diet is often recommended while working on restoring gut health and repopulating the microbiome.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 8}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "RISK ASSESSMENT", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Considerations to assess whether this is the right feeding modality for your dog.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 3, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 3}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "block", "fields": {"id": "68f2998b3aab1650e1c7560b", "tabs": [{"id": "68f299c588024ff279ce7f1f", "rows": [{"id": "690bcfcd67b537641d3cde1e", "cells": [{"id": "690bcfd267b537641d3cde20", "value": "Does your dog struggle with any gastrointestinal diseases?"}, {"id": "690bcfd367b537641d3cde22"}]}, {"id": "690bcfdc67b537641d3cde24", "cells": [{"id": "690bcfe067b537641d3cde26", "value": "Does your dog have a severe illness?"}, {"id": "690bcfe167b537641d3cde28"}]}, {"id": "690bcfe467b537641d3cde2a", "cells": [{"id": "690bcfe867b537641d3cde2c", "value": "Are there immunocompromised family members in your household?"}, {"id": "690bcfe967b537641d3cde2e"}]}, {"id": "690bcfef67b537641d3cde30", "cells": [{"id": "690bcff267b537641d3cde32", "value": "Do you have young children in the house?"}, {"id": "690bcff267b537641d3cde34"}]}, {"id": "690bcff867b537641d3cde36", "cells": [{"id": "690bcffc67b537641d3cde38", "value": "Do you live with elderly?"}, {"id": "690bcffd67b537641d3cde3a"}]}, {"id": "690bd00067b537641d3cde3c", "cells": [{"id": "690bd00367b537641d3cde3e", "value": "Is anyone in your house pregnant?"}, {"id": "690bd00467b537641d3cde40"}]}], "title": "Assessment", "columns": [{"id": "68f299d288024ff279ce7f21", "type": "text", "heading": "Considerations", "mobileWidth": "", "desktopWidth": "", "selectOptions": []}, {"id": "68f299e988024ff279ce7f23", "type": "checkbox", "heading": "Check all that apply", "mobileWidth": "", "desktopWidth": "18%", "selectOptions": []}]}], "maxWidth": "872px", "blockName": "", "blockType": "tabbedTable", "textColor": "#FFFFFF", "borderColor": "#000000", "inactiveTextColor": "#000000", "cellBackgroundColor": "#FCB5A4", "headerBackgroundColor": "#EE6C4D"}, "format": "", "version": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "GLOSSARY", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Feeding a raw diet comes with its own language. First, there are two primary types of raw feeding models: the BARF model raw diet and the PMR style raw diets. Raw feeding models break out a recipe into different percentages of muscle meat, organs, bone, and fiber. Simply following the raw feeding model’s ratios can create a recipe that falls short in essential nutrients like zinc, manganese, vitamin E & D, omega-3s, iodine, and more. These diets require additional supplementation and can cause nutritional deficiencies in your dog if fed long term.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Beyond these feeding models, many pet food companies have \\"kill steps\\" in place like HPP processing, lactic acid baths, and bacteriophaging to neutralize potentially harmful bacteria commonly found on raw meats. Knowing these terms helps you evaluate brands, ask the right questions, and choose the feeding approach that aligns with your individual dog's needs and your household's priorities.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "block", "fields": {"id": "68f186cf577f756260829d51", "items": [{"id": "68f186d12b163d67b225775e", "icon": "sound_detection_dog_barking", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What It Means:", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "BARF stands for Biologically Appropriate Raw Food, hailed by Dr. Ian Billinghurst, longtime raw pet food advocate who is often referred to as the \\"father of raw food.\\" BARF diets have evolved quite a bit since Dr. Billinghurst first began advocating for them.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "The true BARF diet follows a feeding model of 60% raw meaty bone, 15% crushed vegetable, 10% organ meats, and 5% fruit. The remaining 10% is left to supplements to fill nutritional gaps and mimic nutrition that may be found in the feces of other animals. Dr. Billinghurst regularly cites the importance of this component of their diet for improving the health of the microbiome.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What To Know", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Since 1993, when Dr. Billinghurst's first book ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "Give Your Dog a Bone", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 2, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " highlighted the benefits of raw diets, the BARF diet has evolved and taken on many different forms with varying percentages of muscle meat, bone, organs, and produce. The one commonality among all BARF diets: they mostly agree dogs are carnivorous selective omnivores and should eat a meat-based diet with some vegetables and fruits present.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "A common BARF diet model among raw feeders today includes 70% muscle meat, 10% raw meaty bone, 7% vegetables, 5% liver, 5% other secreting organ, 2% seeds or nuts, and 1% fruit. This emphasizes whole-food based nutrition with limited supplementation, where the percentages adjust to meet your individual dog's needs. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}}, "heading": "BARF Diets", "subheading": "Biologically Appropriate Raw Food"}, {"id": "68f186f42b163d67b2257760", "icon": "pets", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What It Means:", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "PMR Diets stand for Prey Model Raw and follow the belief that dogs are carnivores and have no need for plant material. It argues that dogs should eat an entirely meat-centric diet, often with some supplementation in commercial diets. There are a variety of ways to approach these diets:", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "ul", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Whole Prey Diet", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": Offers an animal in its totality to be consumed. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 2, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Franken-Prey Diet", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": 75-80% muscle meat, 10% raw meaty bone, 5% liver, 5% other secreting organs, 0-5% animal-based fiber which often looks like fur, feathers, and the small amount present in bone. In these diets, different parts of the animal are fed without grinding or processing. These meals will often have whole kidneys, hearts, livers, necks, ears, fur, feathers, and feet present.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 3, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "PMR-Style Grinds", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": The most common of these diets, often referred to as “80/10/10.” These include 80% muscle meats, 10% raw meaty bones, and 10% organs finely ground together, of which there are many premade options.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}], "listType": "bullet", "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What To Know", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Some dogs do best on these style diets. Due to the generally limited ingredients, they can make elimination diet processes much smoother in difficult allergy and sensitivity cases.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "PMR Diets", "subheading": "Prey Model Raw"}, {"id": "68f1870f2b163d67b2257762", "icon": "ulna_radius_alt", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What It Means: ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Muscle meat is the primary component of a raw diet and includes more than just boneless muscle. The muscle meat category encompasses ingredients based on their biological functions rather than commercial names. This includes boneless muscle meat, raw animal fat, animal tongues, heart, green tripe (unbleached stomach lining from ruminant animals), lungs, gizzards (the mechanical stomach in poultry), and connective tissues like cartilage and tendons. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What To Know", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Raw diet ratios typically include 60-80% muscle meat, which serves as the main source of protein and also provides water-soluble vitamins and minerals. Examples of boneless muscle meat include ground meat, breast, loin, thigh, heart, gizzards, lung, and more.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Muscle Meat", "subheading": "Boneless Muscle Meat"}, {"id": "68f1871a2b163d67b2257764", "icon": "pet_supplies", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What It Means: ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Raw meaty bones are edible bones covered in raw muscle meat and connective tissues. These bones provide essential calcium and other nutrients that play a vital role in creating firm stool. Raw diet ratios include 10-15% edible bone content for dogs as the main source of calcium. In traditional raw diets, they are fed whole or in ground form. Common options include wings, necks, feet, frames/carcasses, ribs, and tails from appropriate protein sources.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What To Know", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "As with any chew, raw meaty bones come with risk of choking and tooth breakage. Certain raw bones should be avoided: machine-cut bones with sharp edges, turkey drumsticks and drumettes (too dense and splinter when broken), whole beef bones (risk tooth fractures), weight-bearing/recreational bones from large grazing animals, and any cooked bones. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Never feed cooked bones. High-heat cooking bones change the molecular structure of the bone so they are more likely to splinter and hurt your dog.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}}, "heading": "RMB", "subheading": "Raw Meaty Bone"}, {"id": "68f187242b163d67b2257766", "icon": "pulmonology", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What It Means: ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Secreting organs are the most nutrient-dense part of an animal and provide essential nutrients that meat and bone lack. In traditional raw diets, a dog is typically fed 5% liver and 5% other secreting organs. Liver is one of the most concentrated sources of vitamin A and also provides folic acid, B vitamins, iron, and copper. Other secreting organs include kidney (concentrated in selenium and B vitamins), spleen (high in iron and tryptophan), pancreas (provides digestive enzymes), brain (source of DHA), thymus (provides hormones for immune function), testicles, and ovaries. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What To Know", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Some dogs can be sensitive to the recommended amount of organ meat and require lower amounts. Excess beef liver can cause Vitamin A toxicity and potentially iron overload.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Organ Meat", "subheading": "Liver & Other Secreting Organs"}, {"id": "68f187362b163d67b2257768", "icon": "fire_hydrant", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What It Means: ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "HPP stands for High Pressure Pasteurization and uses pressure to kill bacteria. This is among one of the most controversial methods with avid raw feeders, as many believe that the process denatures the food.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "“While I am inclined to agree that any process we put food through does lower the nutritional value of that food in some capacity, I am not against HPP. It is one of the most effective methods for neutralizing salmonella and I do believe it keeps many pets and families safe from exposure.”", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 2, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " – Autumn Somers", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What To Know", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Many companies do their own research on how HPP affects their product and make up for it in the formulation. Others reference research more broadly done on HPP. Steve's Real Food, a raw dog and cat food brand, utilizes HPP and ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"id": "690bd0e07231316a6372932f", "type": "link", "fields": {"url": "https://stevesrealfood.com/2020/01/13/Steves-real%20-foods-study-of-high-pressure-processing-on-raw-meat-diets/", "newTab": false, "linkType": "custom"}, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "cites their findings", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 8, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " on the minimal effects it has on their diets and how they counteract this in their formulation.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "HPP", "subheading": "High Pressure Pasteurization"}, {"id": "68f1873d2b163d67b225776a", "icon": "water_ph", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What It Means: ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Antimicrobial washes utilizing lactic acid create acidity within cells that can reduce the presence of pathogenic bacteria like Salmonella, E. Coli, and Listeria.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What To Know", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "It has been found that both HPP and lactic acid methods are most effective ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"id": "690bd0f97231316a63729330", "type": "link", "fields": {"url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/%20pii/S0362028X24001741", "newTab": false, "linkType": "custom"}, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "when utilized together", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 8, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ". ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Lactic Acid Baths", "subheading": "Antimicrobial Washing"}, {"id": "68f187472b163d67b225776c", "icon": "coronavirus", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What It Means: ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Bacteriophaging is becoming more popular among brands now for its limited effects on the nutritional value of the product. This method uses viruses or phages to neutralize potentially harmful pathogenic bacteria like Salmonella, E. Coli, and Listeria.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What To Know", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Bacteriophaging has been shown to be particularly effective against Salmonella, with ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"id": "690bd1147231316a63729331", "type": "link", "fields": {"url": "http://nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5056775/", "newTab": false, "linkType": "custom"}, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "a 60-92% reduction", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 8, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " on various types of food and produce. This method represents promising developments in raw food safety as it’s a more natural method of control. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Bacteriophaging", "subheading": "Viral Pathogen Control"}], "blockName": "", "blockType": "iconAccordions", "secondaryColorLayout": true}, "format": "", "version": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "CHOOSING A FOOD", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Commercial pet food is regulated by the FDA. The FDA has a zero-tolerance policy for pathogenic bacteria being found on raw pet food. Due to this, many pet food companies have “kill steps” in place. When you make your dog’s raw diet at home, the meats you select are regulated by the USDA. The USDA allows certain amounts of pathogenic bacteria to be present on our meats as they are intended to be cooked. This means when you purchase meat it more than likely has some form of pathogenic bacteria present, and when you feed it to your dog raw they're more than likely being exposed to this potentially harmful bacteria. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "If choosing a pre-made commercial raw pet food, there are various things to consider: the quality of ingredients, the nutrients supplied, and whether you prefer it to be whole-food based nutrition or supplements included. Ingredients in raw pet foods come in various forms of quality. Those that utilize higher quality meats, such as those pasture-raised and fed organic diets, will be more naturally nutrient-dense, supply a lower toxin load, and they will also be more costly because raising animals in this manner is more expensive.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "A study done on pasture-raised eggs and conventionally raised eggs highlights this. The study was conducted out of Pennsylvania University and showed that pasture-raised eggs contain twice as many anti-inflammatory omega-3s, three times as much vitamin D, four times as much vitamin E, and seven times as much beta-carotene when compared to free-range eggs. Due to the differences in raising and, in turn, health, it was found that certified organic meats were 56% less likely to be contaminated with pathogenic bacteria.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Whole-food based diets rely on supplying natural nutrition through whole foods. These are also generally more expensive than supplement-inclusive foods. Supplement-inclusive foods use added vitamins and minerals to meet nutritional requirements. Often more affordable while still providing balanced nutrition. Choosing a food that supplies optimal nutrition for your pet and is also sustainable for your budget are equally as important.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "TRANSITIONING", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "A gradual transition and the cold turkey method are two popular methods of diet transition for dogs. With both transition methods, it's normal for your dog to experience digestive changes for the first two weeks. This can include diarrhea or loose stool, hard stool, and gas. These symptoms can last up to two weeks, but in most cases resolve within the first week. Digestive enzymes and soluble fiber, such as psyllium husk, can help mitigate symptoms during this period.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "It’s also normal to notice your dog reducing their water consumption after transitioning to a raw diet. This happens because most of their water needs are now being met by the high moisture content in the diet.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "There is a lot of fear around feeding raw diets. Regardless of whether you’re considering a commercial raw pet food or homemade raw pet food, it requires the ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"id": "690bd1557231316a63729332", "type": "link", "fields": {"url": "https://www.fda.gov/consumers/womens-health-topics/food-safety-home", "newTab": false, "linkType": "custom"}, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "same safety practices", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 8, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 8}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " you use when preparing raw food for yourself:", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 8}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "ul", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Wash surfaces in hot soapy water", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 2, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Disinfect areas used to prepare the food", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 3, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Never leave food sitting out on the counter", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 4, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Use separate cutting boards and utensils for raw and cooked food", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 5, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Wash your hands with soap and water for at least 20 seconds after handling raw foods", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 6, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Do not feed food left in the fridge for more than 3 days", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 7, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Always clean bowls and utensils in hot soapy water after meals", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}], "listType": "bullet", "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "block", "fields": {"id": "68f18811577f756260829d53", "items": [{"id": "68f188122b163d67b225776e", "icon": "clock_loader_60", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What It Means: ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "This is the most commonly known method, which aims at slowly introducing the new diet to your dog by gradually adding a little bit less of the previous diet and a little bit more of the new diet over a period of several days. It’s generally done over the course of 4-7 days. The idea behind this method is that your dog’s gut is being given the time to adjust to the new diet.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What To Know", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Older dogs who have been fed a processed diet for long periods of time, or dogs that have experienced little variation in diet. If your pet has BVS (Bilious Vomiting Syndrome) or commonly gets bile vomits when hungry, the gradual transition is likely an easier route. This method can be gentler on the stomach as the microbiome readapts to the new food.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}}, "heading": "Gradual Transition Method", "subheading": "Slowly introduce the new diet"}, {"id": "68f1882a2b163d67b2257770", "icon": "nest_clock_farsight_analog", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What It Means: ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "This method includes a 24-hour fasting period, followed by the immediate and total introduction of the new diet as the first meal post-fast.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What To Know", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Certain health cases where you're monitoring the total consumption of certain nutrients like copper.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Cold Turkey Method", "subheading": "Immediately switch foods after a 24-hour fast"}, {"id": "68f188362b163d67b2257772", "icon": "wash", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What It Means: ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "There is a lot of fear around feeding raw diets. Regardless of whether you’re considering a commercial raw pet food or homemade raw pet food, it requires the ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"id": "690bd1a07231316a63729333", "type": "link", "fields": {"url": "https://www.fda.gov/consumers/womens-health-topics/food-safety-home", "newTab": false, "linkType": "custom"}, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "same safety practices", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 8, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " you use when preparing raw food for yourself.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What To Know", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"tag": "ul", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Wash surfaces in hot soapy water", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 2, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Disinfect areas used to prepare the food", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 3, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Never leave food sitting out on the counter", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 4, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Use separate cutting boards and utensils for raw and cooked food", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 5, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Wash your hands with soap and water for at least 20 seconds after handling raw foods", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 6, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Do not feed food left in the fridge for more than 3 days", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 7, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Always clean bowls and utensils in hot soapy water after meals", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}], "listType": "bullet", "direction": null}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Food Safety Tips", "subheading": "Practice safe food handling"}], "blockName": "", "blockType": "iconAccordions", "secondaryColorLayout": false}, "format": "", "version": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "block", "fields": {"id": "68f18898577f756260829d54", "items": [], "heading": "", "blockName": "", "blockType": "collectionItemSlider", "aspectRatio": "aspect-[1.25/1]", "overflowSetting": "md:!overflow-hidden", "usePlaceholders": true, "widthConstraint": true, "placeholderCount": 5, "placeholderImage": 2, "placeholderTitle": "Name"}, "format": "", "version": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 8}}	690b9eb3819f9179409794a2	\N	container-small
3	156	version.layout	1113	{"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "INTRODUCTION", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "While advances in veterinary medicine are extending our dogs' lives in many areas, obesity remains one of the most significant threats to their health and longevity. A 14-year lifetime study researched the effects that diet restriction had on lifespan and age-related changes in dogs. It revealed that overweight and obesity alone reduces dogs' lifespans by at least two years. Maintaining your dog's ideal body condition and muscle mass is one of the most powerful commitments you can make to their health. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Obesity is a disease of chronic inflammation because fat is pro-inflammatory. While we often focus on how excess weight affects joints, the reality is that obesity leads to many other diseases throughout your dog’s body that aren’t always visible. Even a few extra pounds on your dog increases cancer risk and accelerates the development of heart failure, kidney disease, diabetes, and more. Keeping your dog at a healthy, lean body weight helps prevent these conditions and increases their overall quality of life.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "RISK ASSESSMENT", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Check all symptoms you’ve noticed to discuss at your next vet appointment.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 3, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 3}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "block", "fields": {"id": "68f2998b3aab1650e1c7560b", "tabs": [{"id": "68f299c588024ff279ce7f1f", "rows": [{"id": "68f29a6488024ff279ce7f33", "cells": [{"id": "68f29a6688024ff279ce7f35", "value": "Ribs are difficult to feel or buried under a thick fat layer"}, {"id": "68f29a6888024ff279ce7f37"}, {"id": "68f29a7188024ff279ce7f3b", "selectedValue": "Mild"}]}, {"id": "690bc8815641261577378157", "cells": [{"id": "690bc8815641261577378158", "value": "Fat deposits near hips and base of tail"}, {"id": "690bc8815641261577378159"}, {"id": "690bc881564126157737815a", "selectedValue": "Mild"}]}, {"id": "690bc886564126157737815f", "cells": [{"id": "690bc8865641261577378160", "value": "Spine is difficult to feel"}, {"id": "690bc8865641261577378161"}, {"id": "690bc8865641261577378162", "selectedValue": "Mild"}]}, {"id": "690bc88e5641261577378167", "cells": [{"id": "690bc88e5641261577378168", "value": "Hips are rounded or hard to detect"}, {"id": "690bc88e5641261577378169"}, {"id": "690bc88e564126157737816a", "selectedValue": "Mild"}]}, {"id": "690bc8a65641261577378177", "cells": [{"id": "690bc8a65641261577378178", "value": "Flat or sagging belly when viewed from the side"}, {"id": "690bc8a65641261577378179"}, {"id": "690bc8a6564126157737817a", "selectedValue": "Mild"}]}, {"id": "690bc89f564126157737816f", "cells": [{"id": "690bc89f5641261577378170", "value": "Broad or rounded body shape with no visible waist"}, {"id": "690bc89f5641261577378171"}, {"id": "690bc89f5641261577378172", "selectedValue": "Mild"}]}], "title": "OVERWEIGHT", "columns": [{"id": "68f299d288024ff279ce7f21", "type": "text", "heading": "Symptom", "mobileWidth": "", "desktopWidth": "", "selectOptions": []}, {"id": "68f299e988024ff279ce7f23", "type": "checkbox", "heading": "See this?", "mobileWidth": "", "desktopWidth": "18%", "selectOptions": []}, {"id": "68f29a0788024ff279ce7f25", "type": "select", "heading": "How bad?", "mobileWidth": "", "desktopWidth": "18%", "selectOptions": [{"id": "68f29a1688024ff279ce7f27", "label": "Mild", "value": "Mild"}, {"id": "68f29a1f88024ff279ce7f29", "label": "Moderate", "value": "Moderate"}, {"id": "690bc820e102c284db6853d8", "label": "Severe", "value": "Severe"}]}]}, {"id": "690bc8d0564126157737819a", "rows": [{"id": "690bc8d056412615773781a1", "cells": [{"id": "690bc8d056412615773781a2", "value": "Ribs are very prominent"}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781a3"}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781a4", "selectedValue": "Mild"}]}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781a5", "cells": [{"id": "690bc8d056412615773781a6", "value": "Spine is bony or prominent"}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781a7"}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781a8", "selectedValue": "Mild"}]}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781a9", "cells": [{"id": "690bc8d056412615773781aa", "value": "Hips feel bony and sharp"}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781ab"}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781ac", "selectedValue": "Mild"}]}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781ad", "cells": [{"id": "690bc8d056412615773781ae", "value": "Severe tummy tuck when viewed from the side"}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781af"}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781b0", "selectedValue": "Mild"}]}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781b1", "cells": [{"id": "690bc8d056412615773781b2", "value": "Overly narrow waist when viewed from the top"}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781b3"}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781b4", "selectedValue": "Mild"}]}], "title": "UNDERWEIGHT", "columns": [{"id": "690bc8d0564126157737819b", "type": "text", "heading": "Symptom", "mobileWidth": "", "desktopWidth": "", "selectOptions": []}, {"id": "690bc8d0564126157737819c", "type": "checkbox", "heading": "See this?", "mobileWidth": "", "desktopWidth": "18%", "selectOptions": []}, {"id": "690bc8d0564126157737819d", "type": "select", "heading": "How bad?", "mobileWidth": "", "desktopWidth": "18%", "selectOptions": [{"id": "690bc8d0564126157737819e", "label": "Mild", "value": "Mild"}, {"id": "690bc8d0564126157737819f", "label": "Moderate", "value": "Moderate"}, {"id": "690bc8d056412615773781a0", "label": "Severe", "value": "Severe"}]}]}], "maxWidth": "872px", "blockName": "", "blockType": "tabbedTable", "textColor": "#FFFFFF", "borderColor": "#000000", "inactiveTextColor": "#000000", "cellBackgroundColor": "#FCB5A4", "headerBackgroundColor": "#EE6C4D"}, "format": "", "version": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "PREVENTION & EARLY DETECTION", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Obesity is the most common preventable disease. Effective weight management extends far beyond simply measuring food portions. It requires learning how to accurately assess your dog’s body condition, understanding if your dog has predisposed risk factors, and regular monitoring throughout your dog’s life. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Many dog parents struggle to accurately assess their dog’s weight. Weighing your dog every month helps catch unhealthy weight gain early and allows for prompt dietary and lifestyle adjustments, but the scale reading alone doesn't give the complete picture. Body condition and muscle condition scores reveal how that weight is distributed as fat versus muscle mass.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Several factors can increase your dog’s risk of becoming overweight, including their breed, feeding choices, spay/neuter status, and not getting enough daily exercise. Although we can’t control genetics, you can directly influence your dog's weight through daily choices:", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "block", "fields": {"id": "68f186cf577f756260829d51", "items": [{"id": "68f186d12b163d67b225775e", "icon": "search", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Why It Matters", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "An ideal body condition is associated with a decreased risk of obesity-related comorbidities. According to the Association of Pet Obesity Prevention (APOP), dog parents commonly classify their overweight or obese dog as having a normal or ideal body condition. Comparing what you see and feel on your dog to a standard body condition score (BCS) should be part of every routine physical exam and nutrition consultation. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What You Can Do", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Learn how to assess your dog’s body condition at home", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "ul", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Rib check", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " - You should be able to easily feel your pet’s ribs when you gently run your fingers along their side. A healthy-weight pet will have a thin layer of fat you can feel but not see. If you feel a thick layer of fat, your pet may be overweight.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 2, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Waist check", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " - From above, your pet should have a visible waist behind the ribs that tapers slightly toward the hips with a slight indentation near the midsection. If there’s no visible waist or it bulges, they may be overweight.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 3, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Tummy check", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " - From the side, you should see a slight tuck or upward slope in the tummy area. A hanging, bulging, or sagging abdomen can indicate excess abdominal or “belly” fat, which is the most biologically active and concerning fat.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}], "listType": "bullet", "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}}, "heading": "Assess Your Dog’s Body Condition", "subheading": "Check ribs, waist, and tummy to spot excess fat"}, {"id": "68f186f42b163d67b2257760", "icon": "massage", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Why It Matters:", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Dogs with significant muscle loss may appear thinner, even if they have an average amount of fat. The term \\"skinny fat\\" refers to a condition where a dog appears to be thin or at a healthy weight, but has a higher body fat percentage and lower muscle mass. This condition can be misleading because the dog may not have visible fat deposits but still lacks muscle tone. Assessing your dog’s muscle condition score (MCS) involves visualization and gentle examination of your dog's spine, shoulder blades, skull, and hip bones.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What You Can Do", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"tag": "ol", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Learn how to assess your dog’s muscle condition at home", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 2, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"tag": "ul", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 1, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Spine check", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": Run your finger along your dog's spine with your thumb about one thumb's width away to feel the parallel muscles. In dogs with adequate muscle, these bands should be about the same height as the spine. Dogs with muscle loss will have easily visible spine protrusions.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 2, "format": "", "indent": 1, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Shoulder blades check", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": Follow your dog's front legs up to find the shoulder blades and the hard, skinny \\"spine of the scapula\\" bone. In well-muscled dogs, you should feel firm muscle filling the area, while dogs with muscle loss will have a concave, sunken area around the visible bone.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 3, "format": "", "indent": 1, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Skull check", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": Find the bony ridge between your dog's ears at the top of their head, then feel for the temporalis muscles on each side. In healthy dogs, these muscles should feel firm but squeezable. Dogs with muscle loss will have a \\"sunken\\" appearance around visible bone.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 4, "format": "", "indent": 1, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Pelvis & hip check", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": Examine where your dog's hind legs attach to their body. Well-muscled dogs have gluteal and thigh muscles that make the bony prominences difficult to see and feel. In under-muscled dogs, multiple large bony areas become visible and easily felt.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}], "listType": "bullet", "direction": null}], "direction": null}], "listType": "number", "direction": null}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}}, "heading": "Check Your Dog’s Muscle Condition", "subheading": "Feel spine, shoulders, and hips for muscle loss."}, {"id": "68f1870f2b163d67b2257762", "icon": "nest_clock_farsight_analog", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Why It Matters", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Free feeding means leaving food out all day so your dog can eat whenever they want, rather than providing measured portions at set times. This feeding method often leads to overeating because it prevents you from controlling portions and tracking your dog’s daily caloric intake. Research shows that measured, timed meals are more effective for preventing obesity compared to allowing constant access to food. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What You Can Do", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"tag": "ol", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Work with your veterinarian to determine how much food your dog should receive in a day. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 2, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Divide your dog’s daily portion into smaller meals throughout the day to help with satiety during this gradual transition. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 3, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"tag": "ul", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 1, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "For example, if your dog is used to eating whenever they’d like, you may want to start with 5-6 meals per day or 3 meals and 3 snacks during the day. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}], "listType": "bullet", "direction": null}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 3, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "To make sure you don't feed more than what your dog needs, measure out the total daily amount at the beginning of the day and place the food in one container. From this container you can measure out their meals while still leaving some food to use as snacks.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 4, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Eventually, the goal will be to reduce the frequency of these meals to 1-3 per day. The snacks may become smaller and more infrequent.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}], "listType": "number", "direction": null}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}}, "heading": "Feed Timed & Measured Meals", "subheading": "Measured portions beat free feeding all day."}, {"id": "68f1871a2b163d67b2257764", "icon": "calculate", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Why It Matters", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Recommendations on dog food labels or from an online feeding calculator can vary greatly. By following those feeding guidelines, many times you are unknowingly overfeeding or underfeeding your dog. When working on maintaining, gaining, or losing weight, your veterinarian determines your dog’s caloric needs by using this formula: Calories (kcal) per day = 70 x (Your dog’s ideal weight in kg)^¾ x MER. Your dog's resting energy requirement (RER) covers basic functions like breathing and sleeping, while maintenance energy requirement (MER) accounts for all other activities and varies based on factors like age, activity level, reproductive status, and health conditions.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What You Can Do", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ": ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"tag": "ol", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Weigh your dog on a reliable scale, such as your veterinarian’s office", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 2, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Determine your dog’s ideal weight", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 3, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Note if your dog is geriatric, spayed/neutered, or in-tact as this will impact your dog’s metabolic needs", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 4, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Use the formula Calories (kcal) per day = 70 x (Your dog’s ideal weight in kg)^¾ x MER", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 5, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Adjust as needed. These calorie calculations should be considered starting points as every dog's metabolism varies. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 6, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Continue to monitor as your dog ages", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}], "listType": "number", "direction": null}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Calculate Your Dog’s Daily Calories", "subheading": "Food labels vary—work with your vet instead."}, {"id": "68f187242b163d67b2257766", "icon": "scale", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Why It Matters", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Spayed/neutered dogs are almost twice as likely to become overweight or obese compared to intact dogs. The risk is higher for males than females of the same age and breed size. Surgical sterilization alters hormone balance, which slows metabolism and can lead to weight gain if dietary adjustments aren't made. This metabolic change is permanent, requiring lifelong vigilance about caloric intake.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What You Can Do", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"tag": "ol", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Reduce calories", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " by 10-20% immediately after spaying/neutering and monitor body condition closely, as metabolism typically slows after surgery. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 2, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Continue monitoring", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " throughout your dog's life since the increased obesity risk persists.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 3, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Work with your veterinarian ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "to recalculate caloric needs using the appropriate activity factor for altered dogs (1.6 vs 1.8 for intact dogs).", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}], "listType": "number", "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}}, "heading": "Reduce Calories After Spay/Neuter", "subheading": "Cut calories 10-20% after surgery."}, {"id": "68f187362b163d67b2257768", "icon": "pet_supplies", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Why It Matters", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Weight management requires accounting for everything your dog consumes, not just their regular food. Every training treat, chew, and table scrap adds up throughout the day. These treats and other extras are often what contribute most to excess calories despite controlled meal portions.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What You Can Do", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ":", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 1}, {"tag": "ol", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Limit treats to 10%", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " of your dog’s total daily calories", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}], "listType": "number", "direction": null}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}}, "heading": "Limit Treats to 10% Per Day", "subheading": "All treats and extras count toward daily calories."}], "blockName": "", "blockType": "iconAccordions", "secondaryColorLayout": true}, "format": "", "version": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "DIET", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "It’s not only important how much you feed your dog, but the macronutrients you are feeding in each meal. A high fiber/low fat diet is helpful for dogs who are obese prone or dogs who are trying to lose weight. For weight management, reduced-fat diets provide fewer calories per volume, meaning dogs can eat larger portions and feel more satisfied without consuming excessive calories. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "A typical ancestral diet provides 50% of calories from fat and 50% from protein. \\"Reduced fat\\" diets contain less than 50% of calories from fat. These diets include more carbohydrates as calories come from carbs, fat, or protein. Diets with less than 25% of calories coming from fat are considered ultra low-fat diets. Ultra low-fat diets should only be fed temporarily as recovery diets before transitioning to reduce-fat options. Research shows very few animals do well on ultra low-fat diets over time.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "MANAGEMENT", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "There are many reasons why your dog may be overweight, such as their breed, lack of exercise, an underlying health condition, or their diet. It’s important to work with your veterinarian in assessing why your dog may be overweight in order to safely get them to a healthy weight. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "A safe weight loss plan includes working with your veterinarian, gradual changes to your dog’s food intake, and frequent scale weigh-ins. It is not recommended to initiate a weight loss plan or change your dog’s calories alone as rapid weight changes can be dangerous for your dog’s health. Avoid these mistakes when helping your dog lose weight:", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "block", "fields": {"id": "690bcb288b6fae82477830a6", "items": [{"id": "690bcb333d56fa48f0c6df0e", "icon": "block", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Weight loss should be slow and gradual in order to ensure it is safe and sustainable for your dog. Extreme calorie reduction can create nutritional deficiencies and harm your dog's metabolic function. The goal should be to lose no more than about 2% body weight per week. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Too Much Caloric Restriction"}, {"id": "690bcb3c3d56fa48f0c6df10", "icon": "arrow_upward", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "While safely and gradually increasing their exercise is an important piece of losing weight, it is difficult to “outwalk” too much food. Most successful weight-loss journeys require a combination of increased activity and a caloric restriction.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Increasing Exercise Without Decreasing Food Intake"}, {"id": "690bcb483d56fa48f0c6df12", "icon": "close", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Weight loss is and should be gradual. It can be easy to become discouraged if you don’t notice your dog losing weight after committing to the weight loss plan for multiple weeks. Frequent scale weigh-ins can be an objective tool to track gradual weight loss. If your dog’s weight is continuing to increase or isn’t decreasing each month, it may be that there are extra sources of calories that weren’t taken into account, or a custom diet formulated to aid in weight loss may be useful.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Giving Up Too Soon"}, {"id": "690bcb523d56fa48f0c6df14", "icon": "family_group", "content": {"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Ensuring that your entire household supports the plan helps set your dog’s weight loss goal up for success. This may require having conversations with extended family, friends, trainers, dog walkers, and neighbors. While coming from a place of love, that extra treat grandma sneaks in or the last bite of toast your spouse always gives, will undoubtedly counteract the calorie restriction efforts. ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}, "heading": "Not Having Everyone On Board"}], "blockName": "", "blockType": "iconAccordions", "secondaryColorLayout": false}, "format": "", "version": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "block", "fields": {"id": "68f18898577f756260829d54", "items": [], "heading": "", "blockName": "", "blockType": "collectionItemSlider", "aspectRatio": "aspect-[1.25/1]", "overflowSetting": "md:!overflow-hidden", "usePlaceholders": true, "widthConstraint": true, "placeholderCount": 5, "placeholderImage": 2, "placeholderTitle": "Name"}, "format": "", "version": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}	690ba02c819f9179409794fa	\N	container-small
3	142	version.layout	921	{"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "PLACEHOLDER", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Sed tincidunt tortor ac egestas laoreet. Quisque nec odio fringilla, varius felis et, eleifend ex. Fusce luctus vitae metus ut volutpat. Donec maximus in tortor sed semper. Nunc libero augue, iaculis eu magna pellentesque, lacinia commodo lacus. Curabitur porta eu sem et lobortis.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Vestibulum maximus eleifend nulla et laoreet. Interdum et malesuada fames ac ante ipsum primis in faucibus. Fusce gravida, magna quis dignissim sodales, libero elit convallis arcu, ut tristique magna sapien vitae quam. Integer metus sem, ornare in aliquet luctus, rutrum eu leo. Donec ullamcorper elit massa, eu elementum augue dignissim et.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "PLACEHOLDER", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textFormat": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "format